       }
      ],
      "text": "Role in ADPKD:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " strict BP control is essential, typically w an ACEi or ARB; they also block angiotensin and aldosterone, growth factors that contribute to renal scarring and loss of renal funct."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Valsartan:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " K-sparing diuretics, K supplements, K-containing salt substitutes or heparin can raise K (and creatinine in HF); dual RAS blockade (ARB + ACE-I or aliskiren) raises hypotension, hyperkalemia and renal-failure risk."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
